<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_header-files">
  <title>Header Files and Header Guards</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, our complete examples have placed a class definition, <c>main</c>,
      and the member-function definitions in one source file. That works well
      for small programs, but larger programs are easier to organize when a
      type's interface is separated from its implementation.
    </p>

    <p>
      A common C++ organization uses three files:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          a <term>header file</term> containing the class definition and member
          function declarations,
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          an <term>implementation file</term> containing the member-function
          definitions, and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          a source file containing <c>main</c> and the code that uses the class.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      We will use the <c>Rectangle</c> class from the previous sections to see
      how these pieces fit together.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Header File</title>

    <p>
      The header file describes the class interface that other source files
      need to know about. A header commonly uses the same base name as the
      class, followed by <c>.h</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <c>Rectangle</c>, we can create a file named <c>Rectangle.h</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#ifndef RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED
#define RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED

class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle();
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

    double calculate_area() const;

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};

#endif
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The class definition belongs in the header because code that creates or
      uses a <c>Rectangle</c> needs to know what a <c>Rectangle</c> is and what
      public operations are available.
    </p>

    <p>
      The member-function bodies do not need to appear here. The declarations
      tell the compiler what operations exist; their definitions can be placed
      in a separate implementation file.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Header Guards</title>

    <p>
      The three preprocessor lines surrounding the class definition form a
      <term>header guard</term>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#ifndef RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED
#define RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED

// contents of Rectangle.h

#endif
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      A header can sometimes be reached more than once while the compiler is
      processing a source file. Without protection, that could cause the same
      class definition to be seen repeatedly.
    </p>

    <p>
      The guard works by defining a unique preprocessor name the first time the
      header is included:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>#ifndef RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED</c> asks whether that name has
          <em>not</em> already been defined.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>#define RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED</c> defines the name the first time
          the header is processed.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>#endif</c> marks the end of the protected contents.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If the same header is encountered again while compiling that source
      file, the protected contents are skipped.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        The guard name should be unique within the project. A descriptive name
        such as <c>RECTANGLE_H_INCLUDED</c> makes accidental collisions less
        likely.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      Many compilers also support
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#pragma once
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      as a convenient alternative. It is widely supported, but traditional
      header guards are part of long-established portable C++ practice, so we
      will use them in this book.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Implementation File</title>

    <p>
      The member-function definitions can be placed in
      <c>Rectangle.cpp</c>. The implementation file includes its own header so
      the compiler sees the class definition before the member-function
      definitions.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#include "Rectangle.h"

Rectangle::Rectangle()
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
}

Rectangle::Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
    if (initial_length > 0.0 && initial_width > 0.0) {
        length = initial_length;
        width = initial_width;
    }
}

double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Because these definitions appear outside the class definition, the scope
      resolution operator <c>::</c> is required, just as it was in the
      previous sections.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice that <c>Rectangle.cpp</c> does not repeat the class definition.
      It includes <c>Rectangle.h</c> and supplies the definitions promised by
      the declarations in that header.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>The File Containing main</title>

    <p>
      A source file that wants to use <c>Rectangle</c> also includes the
      header. For example, <c>main.cpp</c> could contain:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

#include "Rectangle.h"

int main() {
    Rectangle unit_square;
    Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

    std::cout << unit_square.calculate_area() << '\n';
    std::cout << room.calculate_area() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      <c>main.cpp</c> needs the class declaration so that the compiler knows
      how objects such as <c>room</c> can be created and which public member
      functions may be called.
    </p>

    <p>
      The complete project now has this organization:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle.h      class definition and member-function declarations
Rectangle.cpp    member-function definitions
main.cpp         program that uses Rectangle
    ]]></program>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Quotes and Angle Brackets in include Directives</title>

    <p>
      We have already used standard-library headers such as
      <c>&lt;iostream&gt;</c>. Our own header uses quotation marks instead:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include "Rectangle.h"
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      As a practical rule:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          use angle brackets for standard-library and other installed library
          headers, such as <c>&lt;iostream&gt;</c> and
          <c>&lt;string&gt;</c>;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          use quotation marks for headers that are part of your own project,
          such as <c>"Rectangle.h"</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The exact search rules are controlled by the compiler and its options,
      but this convention communicates whether a header belongs to the project
      or to an installed library.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Header Should Include What It Needs</title>

    <p>
      A header should include the standard-library headers required by the
      declarations it contains. For example, suppose a class stores and
      exposes a <c>std::string</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
#ifndef BOOK_H_INCLUDED
#define BOOK_H_INCLUDED

#include <string>

class Book {
public:
    Book(const std::string& initial_title);

private:
    std::string title;
};

#endif
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      <c>Book.h</c> uses <c>std::string</c>, so <c>Book.h</c> itself includes
      <c>&lt;string&gt;</c>. It should not depend on some other source file
      happening to include <c>&lt;string&gt;</c> first.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is another application of the rule <q>include what you use</q>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Compiling Multiple Source Files</title>

    <p>
      The header file is included by the source files, but it is normally not
      listed as a separate source file in the compile command. To build this
      three-file project with <c>g++</c>, compile the two <c>.cpp</c> files
      together:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic Rectangle.cpp main.cpp -o rectangle_program
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The compiler processes the source files and the linker combines the
      compiled pieces into one executable program.
    </p>

    <p>
      On Windows, the resulting executable may be run as:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
.\rectangle_program.exe
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      On Linux or macOS, it may be run as:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
./rectangle_program
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Development tools and IDEs can perform these compile and link steps for
      you, but understanding that a project can contain several source files is
      an important part of understanding C++ programs.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Why Separate the Files?</title>

    <p>
      Splitting a small program into three files may initially seem like extra
      work. The benefits become more important as programs grow.
    </p>

    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Clear interface</title>
        <p>
          The header shows what other code needs to know about the type without
          requiring readers to inspect every implementation detail.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Organization</title>
        <p>
          The class implementation can be developed separately from the code
          that uses the class.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Reuse</title>
        <p>
          Several source files or programs can include the same class
          declaration and use the same implementation.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Separate compilation</title>
        <p>
          In larger projects, source files can be compiled separately and
          linked together. Build tools can avoid recompiling source files that
          have not changed.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>

    <p>
      This separation also reinforces an idea from the previous sections:
      code that uses an object should depend primarily on its public interface,
      while the implementation details can remain elsewhere.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_header_files_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which file normally contains the <c>Rectangle</c> class definition
          and its member-function declarations?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>Rectangle.h</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The header provides the class definition and declarations needed
              by source files that use <c>Rectangle</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>Rectangle.cpp</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The implementation file normally contains the member-function
              definitions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>main.cpp</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>main.cpp</c> can use the class by including its header, but
              the class definition belongs in the header in this organization.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_header_files_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which directive is the usual way to include a header that belongs to
          your own project?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>#include &lt;Rectangle.h&gt;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Angle brackets are normally used for standard-library and other
              installed library headers.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>#include "Rectangle.h"</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Quotation marks are the usual form for a header that belongs to
              the current project.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>#include Rectangle.h</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The header name must be enclosed in quotation marks or angle
              brackets.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_header_files_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the main purpose of a header guard?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It prevents other source files from using the class.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A header is intended to be shared by source files that need its
              declarations.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It prevents the protected header contents from being processed
              repeatedly when the same header is encountered more than once
              while compiling a source file.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The guard allows the header contents to be processed once for
              that compilation and skipped on later encounters.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It automatically makes all class data private.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Access control is determined by <c>public:</c>,
              <c>private:</c>, and the type's default access rules.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_header_files_4"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks to create the basic protected contents of
          <c>Robot.h</c>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>#ifndef ROBOT_H_INCLUDED</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>#define ROBOT_H_INCLUDED</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>class Robot {</cline>
          <cline>public:</cline>
          <cline>    Robot();</cline>
          <cline>private:</cline>
          <cline>    int battery_level;</cline>
          <cline>};</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="4">
          <cline>#endif</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>#include ROBOT_H_INCLUDED</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>#ifndef "Robot.h"</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_header_files_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A file named <c>Book.h</c> contains this declaration:
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
class Book {
public:
    Book(const std::string& initial_title);

private:
    std::string title;
};
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          Which standard-library header should <c>Book.h</c> include itself?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>&lt;iostream&gt;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The declarations shown do not use stream types or
              <c>std::cout</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>&lt;string&gt;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The header directly uses <c>std::string</c>, so it should include
              <c>&lt;string&gt;</c> itself.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p>No header is needed.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A header should include the declarations required by the types it
              uses rather than depending on another file to include them first.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
